# ☁️ Day — Amazon CloudFront

Today I learned about **Amazon CloudFront**, AWS's Content Delivery Network (CDN) service. I learned how CloudFront delivers static and dynamic content to users through globally distributed **Edge Locations**, helping reduce latency and improve application performance.

## 🔹 What is Amazon CloudFront?

Amazon CloudFront is a **fast and secure Content Delivery Network (CDN)** provided by AWS. It delivers content such as HTML, CSS, JavaScript, images, videos, and APIs to users from locations closer to them.

Instead of every user request traveling directly to the origin server, CloudFront can cache frequently requested content at AWS Edge Locations and serve it from the nearest available location.

## 🔹 Why do we need CloudFront?

Without CloudFront:

```text
User
  ↓
Origin Server / S3
  ↓
Content
```

Every request may need to travel to the origin, which can increase latency for users who are geographically far away.

With CloudFront:

```text
User
  ↓
Nearest Edge Location
  ↓
Cached Content
```

If the requested content is already cached, CloudFront can serve it directly from the Edge Location instead of requesting it again from the origin.

## 🔹 CloudFront Architecture

```text
                    Users
                 /    |    \
                ↓     ↓     ↓
          Edge Locations
                |
                ↓
        Amazon CloudFront
                |
                ↓
             Origin
          /           \
         ↓             ↓
       S3             EC2
                      / 
                    ALB
```

The **origin** is the location from which CloudFront retrieves content when it is not already available in the cache.

## 🔹 Important CloudFront Components

### 1. Distribution

A **CloudFront Distribution** is the main configuration that tells CloudFront where to get content from and how to deliver it to users.

It defines things such as:

* Origin
* Cache behavior
* Allowed HTTP methods
* HTTPS configuration
* Viewer protocol policy
* Geographic restrictions
* Logging and other settings

### 2. Origin

The origin is the backend source of the content.

Examples include:

* Amazon S3
* Application Load Balancer
* EC2
* API Gateway
* Other HTTP servers

For my hands-on practice, I worked with a **static website hosted on Amazon S3**.

### 3. Edge Locations

CloudFront uses a global network of Edge Locations.

When a user requests content, CloudFront can serve cached content from an Edge Location that is geographically closer to the user.

### 4. Cache

CloudFront temporarily stores frequently requested content at Edge Locations.

For example:

```text
User 1 → CloudFront → S3
                 ↓
              Cache

User 2 → CloudFront
           ↓
      Cached Content
```

The second request may be served from the cache without contacting S3 again.

## 🔹 CloudFront with Amazon S3

A common architecture for static websites is:

```text
User
  ↓
CloudFront
  ↓
S3 Bucket
  ↓
HTML / CSS / JS / Images
```

I learned that S3 can act as the **origin** while CloudFront acts as the **CDN layer** in front of it.

This provides a way to distribute static content globally while reducing the number of requests reaching the S3 origin.

## 🔹 Cache Hit and Cache Miss

I also learned the basic caching concepts.

### Cache Hit

If the requested object already exists in the CloudFront cache:

```text
User
 ↓
CloudFront Edge Location
 ↓
Cached Content
```

CloudFront can return the content directly.

### Cache Miss

If the object is not available in the cache:

```text
User
 ↓
CloudFront
 ↓
Origin (S3)
 ↓
Content
 ↓
CloudFront Cache
 ↓
User
```

CloudFront retrieves the object from the origin and can cache it for subsequent requests according to the configured caching behavior.

## 🔹 TTL

**TTL (Time To Live)** controls how long CloudFront keeps an object in its cache before it needs to check the origin again.

For example:

```text
TTL = 1 hour
```

The cached object can remain fresh according to that configured caching policy for that period.

## 🔹 HTTPS and Security

CloudFront supports secure content delivery using **HTTPS**.

It can also integrate with AWS security services and features such as:

* AWS WAF
* AWS Shield
* Origin Access Control (OAC)
* TLS/SSL certificates through AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)

For an S3 origin, **Origin Access Control (OAC)** can be used so that CloudFront accesses the S3 bucket on behalf of users rather than requiring the bucket to be publicly accessible.

## 🔹 CloudFront vs S3

I learned that S3 and CloudFront solve different problems.

**Amazon S3:**

> Object storage used to store files such as HTML, CSS, JavaScript, images, backups, and other objects.

**Amazon CloudFront:**

> CDN used to deliver content efficiently to users through a global network of edge locations.

Together:

```text
S3
↓
Stores the website

CloudFront
↓
Delivers the website
```

## 🔹 Main Benefits of CloudFront

* Reduces latency by serving cached content closer to users
* Improves website and application performance
* Reduces repeated requests to the origin
* Supports HTTPS and secure content delivery
* Provides caching and customizable cache behaviors
* Integrates with S3, EC2, ALB, API Gateway, and other origins
* Works with AWS security services such as WAF and Shield
* Provides global content distribution

